<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-external-force-setup" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Setting Up the Forced Undamped System</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       INTRO PROSE: RECAP FREE UNDAMPED SYSTEM
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    In <xref ref="sec-undamped-mass-spring"/>, we modeled a mass <m>m</m> attached
    to a spring with spring constant <m>k</m>, with no friction or damping. Newton's
    second law gave the <term>free undamped</term> mass-spring ODE:
    <me>mx'' + kx = 0,</me>
    or equivalently, writing <m>\omega_0 = \sqrt{k/m}</m>
    (see <xref ref="def-natural-frequency"/>),
    <me>x'' + \omega_0^2 x = 0.</me>
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       MC CHECKPOINT: SOLUTION TO FREE UNDAMPED ODE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Before adding anything new, let's make sure the solution to this equation
    is fresh. Recall <xref ref="obs-simple-harmonic-ode"/> if you need a reminder.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mc-free-undamped-solution">
    <statement>
      <p>
        What is the general solution to <m>x'' + \omega_0^2 x = 0</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p><m>x = c_1\cos(\omega_0 t) + c_2\sin(\omega_0 t)</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct! The characteristic equation is <m>r^2 + \omega_0^2 = 0</m>,
            giving complex roots <m>r = \pm\omega_0 i</m>, so the general solution
            is a combination of cosine and sine at frequency <m>\omega_0</m>.
            <m>\checkmark</m>
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>x = c_1 e^{\omega_0 t} + c_2 e^{-\omega_0 t}</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Exponentials would arise from real roots, but the characteristic equation
            <m>r^2 + \omega_0^2 = 0</m> gives <m>r = \pm\omega_0 i</m> — purely
            imaginary roots. That means trig functions, not exponentials.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>x = c_1\cos(\omega_0^2 t) + c_2\sin(\omega_0^2 t)</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Close, but the frequency inside the trig functions is <m>\omega_0</m>,
            not <m>\omega_0^2</m>. The roots are <m>r = \pm\omega_0 i</m>,
            so the argument is <m>\omega_0 t</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>x = c_1\cos(t) + c_2\sin(t)</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            This would be the solution to <m>x'' + x = 0</m> (with <m>\omega_0 = 1</m>).
            In general <m>\omega_0 = \sqrt{k/m}</m> is not necessarily 1, so
            the frequency inside the trig functions must be <m>\omega_0</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       BRIDGE PROSE: ADD EXTERNAL FORCE, DERIVE ODE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    That was <em>free</em> motion: the only forces acting on the mass were
    the spring restoring force <m>F_S = -kx</m> and (in the damped case) friction.
    Now suppose an additional external force <m>F_E(t)</m> is applied to the mass 
    (think of someone pushing and pulling the block rhythmically). Newton's second
    law now reads
    <me>ma = F_S + F_E,</me>
    and since <m>F_S = -kx</m> and <m>a = x''</m>, rearranging gives
    <me>mx'' + kx = F_E(t).</me>
    We focus on the <term>undamped</term> case (no dashpot, so <m>c = 0</m>),
    and we take the external force to be sinusoidal:
    <me>F_E(t) = F_0\cos(\omega t) + G_0\sin(\omega t),</me>
    where <m>F_0</m>, <m>G_0</m>, and <m>\omega</m> are constants.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       DEFINITION: THE FORCED UNDAMPED ODE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <definition xml:id="def-forced-undamped-ode">
    <title>The Forced Undamped Mass-Spring ODE</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The position <m>x(t)</m> of an undamped mass-spring system subject to
        a sinusoidal external force satisfies:
        <me>mx'' + kx = F_0\cos(\omega t) + G_0\sin(\omega t).</me>
        Dividing both sides by <m>m</m> and writing <m>\omega_0 = \sqrt{k/m}</m>:
        <me>x'' + \omega_0^2 x = \frac{F_0}{m}\cos(\omega t) + \frac{G_0}{m}\sin(\omega t).</me>
        Here:
        <ul>
          <li><p>
            <m>\omega_0 = \sqrt{k/m}</m> is the <term>natural frequency</term> of
            the system: the frequency it oscillates at when left alone.
          </p></li>
          <li><p>
            <m>\omega</m> is the <term>forcing frequency</term>: the frequency of
            the applied external force.
          </p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       PROSE: x_c AND WHY omega_0 IS "NATURAL"
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Since this is a non-homogeneous ODE (just like in
    <xref ref="sec-general-solution-nonhomogeneous"/>), its general solution
    will have the form <m>x = x_c + x_p</m>, where <m>x_p</m> is a particular
    solution and <m>x_c</m> is the <term>complementary solution</term>: the
general solution to the homogeneous equation
<m>x'' + \omega_0^2 x = 0</m>, i.e., the system <em>with no external force</em>.
    From the checkpoint above:
    <me>x_c = c_1\cos(\omega_0 t) + c_2\sin(\omega_0 t).</me>
    This is exactly why <m>\omega_0</m> is called the <em>natural</em> frequency:
    it is the frequency at which the system freely oscillates on its own,
    with no outside influence. The forcing frequency <m>\omega</m>, by contrast,
    is imposed from the outside.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       REMARK: CIRCULAR FREQUENCY
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <remark xml:id="remark-circular-frequency">
    <title>Circular vs. ordinary frequency</title>
    <p>
      Technically, <m>\omega_0</m> and <m>\omega</m> are <term>circular frequencies</term>,
      measured in radians per unit time rather than cycles per unit time (which
      would require a factor of <m>2\pi</m>). In this context we will simply
      say <q>frequency</q> and mean the circular version throughout.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       KEY TAKEAWAYS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <conclusion>
    <title>Key Takeaways</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>
          <alert>The ODE:</alert> Adding a sinusoidal external force to the
          undamped mass-spring system gives
          <m>x'' + \omega_0^2 x = \frac{F_0}{m}\cos(\omega t) + \frac{G_0}{m}\sin(\omega t)</m>,
          a non-homogeneous ODE we now have the tools to solve.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Two frequencies:</alert> <m>\omega_0 = \sqrt{k/m}</m> is the
          natural frequency (how the system oscillates freely);
          <m>\omega</m> is the forcing frequency (the rhythm imposed from outside).
          Whether these are equal or not will turn out to matter enormously.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Complementary solution:</alert>
          <m>x_c = c_1\cos(\omega_0 t) + c_2\sin(\omega_0 t)</m> comes from the
          free system, and <m>x_p</m> will come from the external force via
          undetermined coefficients. We find <m>x_p</m> on the next page.
        </p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </conclusion>

